Q: How to map a list of products in source JSON to a single product in target based on unique ID? A: <p>Your problem boils down to the question: How can you inject a Mapper into the base definition that isn't being used in the implementation?</p>
<p>The only way to achieve that is:</p>
<ul>
<li>Switch the base definition from an interface to an abstract class.</li>
<li>Inject the desired Mapper using field injection.</li>
</ul>
<p>So in your case:</p>
<pre><code>@Mapper(componentModel = MappingConstants.ComponentModel.SPRING)
public abstract class MainMapper {
  @Autowired
  private ProductMapper productMapper;

[and so on...]
</code></pre>
<p>EDIT:</p>
<p>Option 1:</p>
<p>However, in many cases you can work around this issue by splitting out the method(s) using the injected Mapper into a separate component and adding that component back into the main Mapper via the <code>uses</code> attribute.</p>
<p>Something like this:</p>
<pre><code>@Component
public class ProductExtractor {
    public static final String MAP_PRODUCT = &quot;mapProduct&quot;;

    private final ProductMapper productMapper;

    public ProductExtractor(final ProductMapper productMapper) {
        this.productMapper = productMapper;
    }

    @Named(MAP_PRODUCT)
    public Product mapProduct(SourceDto dto) {
        ProductDto desiredProduct = filteredProduct(dto);
        return productMapper.toProduct(desiredProduct);
    }

    private ProductDto filteredProduct(SourceDto dto) {
        String desiredId = &quot;numberFromConfig&quot;;
        List&lt;ProductDto&gt; products= dto.getProducts();
        return products.stream()
                .filter(product -&gt; product.getId().equals(desiredId))
                .findAny()
                .get();
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The Mapper would then look somewhat like this:</p>
<pre><code>@Mapper(componentModel = MappingConstants.ComponentModel.SPRING, uses = ProductExtractor.class )
public interface MainMapper {
    @Mapping(target = &quot;product&quot;, source = &quot;.&quot;, qualifiedByName = ProductExtractor.MAP_PRODUCT)
    Target map(SourceDto dto);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Option 2:</p>
<p>Alternatively, you could try to work around this limitation with a <a href="https://mapstruct.org/documentation/stable/reference/html/#customizing-mappers-using-decorators" rel="nofollow noreferrer">decorator</a>:</p>
<pre><code>@DecoratedWith(MainMapperDecorator.class)
@Mapper(componentModel = MappingConstants.ComponentModel.SPRING)
public interface MainMapper {
    @Mapping(target = &quot;product&quot;, ignore = true)
    Target map(SourceDto dto);
}
</code></pre>
<pre><code>@Component
public abstract class MainMapperDecorator implements MainMapper {
    private final MainMapper delegateMapper;
    private final ProductMapper productMapper;

    public MainMapperDecorator (@org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Qualifier(&quot;delegate&quot;)
        MainMapper delegateMapper, ProductMapper productMapper)
} {
        this.delegateMapper = delegateMapper;
        this.ProductMapper = productMapper;
    }

    @Override
    public Target map(SourceDto dto) {
        Target target = delegateMapper.map(dto);
        ProductDto desiredProduct = filteredProduct(dto);
        target.product = productMapper.mapProduct(desiredProduct);
    }

    private ProductDto filteredProduct(SourceDto dto) {
        String desiredId = &quot;numberFromConfig&quot;;
        List&lt;ProductDto&gt; products= dto.getProducts();
        return products.stream()
                .filter(product -&gt; product.getId().equals(desiredId))
                .findAny()
                .get();
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>You mentioned in your post that the real mapping is somewhat more complex; so I can't be 100% sure whether both these options apply to your use case and/or which one will be the &quot;better&quot; one. But I'd be quite surprised if you can't apply at least one of them (with a little refactoring if need be).</p>

Q: Encode video from C++ using libavcodec and VAAPI A: <p>Maybe a bit late, but for anyone still interested, the original code (without filtering API) actually works, but gretel99 missed out on doing av_hwframe_ctx_alloc, see the FFMmpeg example in <a href="https://ffmpeg.org/doxygen/trunk/vaapi__transcode_8c_source.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">doc/examples/vaapi_transcode.c</a>.</p>
<p>Here's a fixed Encoder::setupEncoder() version with comments what need's to be changed. It's not only the missing hwframe buffer allocation, at least for my VAAPI driver rc_mode=CQP is required and global_quality must be set to shut up a warning.</p>
<pre><code>void Encoder::setupEncoder()
{
    const char* encoderName = m_hardwareAcceleration? &quot;h264_vaapi&quot;: &quot;libx264&quot;;
    AVCodec* videoCodec = avcodec_find_encoder_by_name(encoderName);
    m_encoder = avcodec_alloc_context3(videoCodec);
    m_encoder-&gt;bit_rate = s_width * s_height * s_fps * 2;
    m_encoder-&gt;width = s_width;
    m_encoder-&gt;height = s_height;
    m_encoder-&gt;time_base = (AVRational){1, s_fps};
    m_encoder-&gt;framerate = (AVRational){s_fps, 1};

    m_encoder-&gt;gop_size = s_fps;  // have at least 1 I-frame per second
    m_encoder-&gt;max_b_frames = 1;
    m_encoder-&gt;pix_fmt = AV_PIX_FMT_YUV420P;

    if(m_hardwareAcceleration) {
        m_encoder-&gt;pix_fmt = AV_PIX_FMT_VAAPI;
        m_encoder-&gt;get_format = get_vaapi_format;

        assert(av_hwdevice_ctx_create(&amp;m_device, AV_HWDEVICE_TYPE_VAAPI, &quot;/dev/dri/renderD128&quot;, nullptr, 0) == 0);

        AVHWDeviceContext* deviceCtx = (AVHWDeviceContext*) m_device-&gt;data;
        assert(deviceCtx-&gt;type == AV_HWDEVICE_TYPE_VAAPI);

        // Fix error: Mismatching AVCodecContext.pix_fmt and AVHWFramesContext.format
        // See doc/examples/vaapi_transcode.c &quot;set_hwframe_ctx()&quot;
        {
            AVBufferRef *hw_frames_ref;
            AVHWFramesContext *frames_ctx = NULL;

            assert((hw_frames_ref = av_hwframe_ctx_alloc(m_device)) != nullptr);
            frames_ctx = (AVHWFramesContext *)(hw_frames_ref-&gt;data);
            frames_ctx-&gt;format    = AV_PIX_FMT_VAAPI;
            frames_ctx-&gt;sw_format = AV_PIX_FMT_NV12;
            frames_ctx-&gt;width     = s_width;
            frames_ctx-&gt;height    = s_height;
            frames_ctx-&gt;initial_pool_size = 20;
            assert(av_hwframe_ctx_init(hw_frames_ref) == 0);
            m_encoder-&gt;hw_frames_ctx = av_buffer_ref(hw_frames_ref);
            assert(m_encoder-&gt;hw_frames_ctx != nullptr);
        }

        // Fix error: Driver does not support any RC mode compatible with selected options (supported modes: CQP).
        assert(av_opt_set(m_encoder-&gt;priv_data, &quot;rc_mode&quot;, &quot;CQP&quot;, AV_OPT_SEARCH_CHILDREN) == 0);

        // Fix warning, cosmetical only: No quality level set; using default (20).
        m_encoder-&gt;global_quality = 20;

        m_encoder-&gt;hw_device_ctx = av_hwframe_ctx_alloc(m_device);
        //m_encoder-&gt;hw_frames_ctx = av_buffer_ref(m_device);           // Fix: Not required, done by av_hwframe_ctx_alloc
        m_hwFrame = av_frame_alloc();
        av_hwframe_get_buffer(m_encoder-&gt;hw_frames_ctx, m_hwFrame, 0); // Fix: Must pass hw_frames_ctx, not m_encoder-&gt;hw_device_ctx
        assert(m_hwFrame != nullptr);
    }

    assert(avcodec_open2(m_encoder, videoCodec, nullptr) == 0);  // &lt;-- returns -22 (EINVAL) for hardware encoder

    m_muxer-&gt;video_codec_id = videoCodec-&gt;id;
    m_muxer-&gt;video_codec = videoCodec;
}
</code></pre>
